summary_nl:
Electronics penetrate more and more into human society. From large mainframe computers, to business use, home environment and personal devices, electronics are now becoming ubiquitous, unobtrusive, wearable and implantable under the umbrella called Internet-of-Things [1]. Due to the massive scale of deployment, these electronics should be designed for the lowest possible costs, with smallest possible form factor and lowest ecological footprint.

Fortunately, integrated electronic circuits have benefitted from CMOS technology scaling during the past >40 years following Moore's exponential trend [2]. This enabled rapid progress in terms of: more complex chip functionality; reduction of power consumption; and reduction of production costs and environmental resources thanks to smaller silicon area. However, this trend only applies to digital circuits that are technology limited and not to analog circuits that are limited by physical properties such as noise and matching. Therefore, analog circuits can dominate overall costs, physical size and power consumption of integrated electronics. Hybrid alternatives such as time-based, oscillation-based or digital-intensive approaches to replace traditional analog circuits have been researched and prototypes were implemented. While those approaches offer certain advantages (e.g. in versatility and voltage scalability), they are not necessarily better in terms of power consumption or chip area. In practice, the limits and trade-offs for analog/hybrid circuits are currently not sufficiently understood, and no design paradigms for hybrid systems achieving minimum power and minimum costs (in terms of chip area) do exist. The objectives of this project are therefore to:

-investigate limits and trade-offs of analog/hybrid circuits in terms of power and size;

-benchmark existing analog and hybrid approaches;

-propose new design methods and architectures for minimum power and area. We especially propose to look into “burst-mode, adaptable, discrete-time analog circuits”;

-implement prototypes to prove the concepts and to support utilization in collaboration with the commercial partners.

